# Symbols that collide between two I values of the siever.
#
# I is a compile-time constant: recurrence6 is built with -DI_bits, the sieve
# kernels are m4-generated with -Dn_i_bits, and the assembly reads the
# mpqs_*/mpqs3_* globals by name.  The six per-I libraries therefore define
# the same symbols and ld refuses to link two of them, or -- worse, because
# archives only contribute the members that resolve an undefined reference --
# silently keeps just one copy:
#
#   multiple definition of `lasched_1'
#
# In a per-I build every name below gets an I suffix, so all six I values share
# one image.  C gets it through -D: the preprocessor renames the definition,
# the declaration and the call site together, and because it works on whole
# tokens, lasched is not disturbed by lasched0.  m4 gets it through -D, which
# is how m4 defines a macro.  Note that the flags have to sit before the first
# input file: GNU m4 stops parsing options at the first non-option argument, and
# in a recipe $(foreach ...) needs a single $ -- $$ hands it to the shell as a
# command substitution, which quietly expands to nothing.
#
# Two sources went into this list, and the second one was the point of a long
# debugging session.  The first was the file-level globals of the 19 per-I
# sources plus the names the assembly reads or defines by name.  The second is
# the 30 names below marked "library label": the assembly's own global labels,
# which nothing in the C sources mentions.  Without them every
# liblasieveI<n>.a still exported lasched, slinie, tdslinie and friends bare,
# so the linker kept a single copy of each kernel; I=12..16 then ran I=11's
# kernel against buffers sized for their own I and died writing through a
# pointer table -- the same crash address as before the rename was in place at
# all.
#
# Found by measuring, not by reading: nm over the I=11 and I=12 copies of
# objI<n>/, asm/liblasieveI<n>.a and asm/libcoreI<n>.a, keeping the names still
# defined without a suffix in both.  Local labels (lowercase in nm) are left
# alone; they cannot collide across objects.
# The list below is measured, not guessed.  Take the six per-I object sets --
# objI<n>/, asm/liblasieveI<n>.a, asm/libcoreI<n>.a -- read every global they
# define with nm, strip the I suffix, and keep the names all six share.  A
# name already suffixed comes back to its base; a name never renamed is
# already its own base.  Both kinds have to be here.
#
# An earlier hand-built list covered the C file-level globals plus the names
# the assembly reads or defines by name, and missed the global functions in
# lasieve-prepn.c and tinyecm.c (rem_epu32_x16, modmul32_16, modinv_16,
# modsub32_16, tecm_mm512_*).  Incremental links did not expose it; a clean
# build did, as multiple definitions between objI11 and objI12.
#
# Local labels (lowercase in nm) are left out: they cannot collide across
# objects.  Nor are the non-per-I objects: lasieve_ctx.o, lasieve_dispatch.o,
# gmp-aux.o and mpz-ull.o are compiled without the rename, so a name any of
# them references must not appear below or the link breaks.
#
